## INTRODUCTION
Aggregation-induced emission (AIE) is an attractive phenomenon as the materials exhibit strong luminescence in the aggregated or solid states, whereas the emission disappears in their dissolved state.1-3 Tang and coworkers investigated the phenomenon and mechanism of AIE systematically.2 Generally, AIE luminogens ( AIEgens ) have a propeller-shaped molecular structure and have been developed with silole,4 tetraphenylethene,5 distyrylanthracene,6 and triphenylethene.7 In the solution state, AIEgens undergo intramolecular rotation. Thus, their excited states suffer from non-radiative annihilation, which leads to weak emission and even non-luminescence. However, the rotation is considerably restricted in solid state or poor solvents.8,9 The restriction of intramolecular rotation (RIR) enhances the emission as rotation-restricted emission (RRE).1-3 AIEgens overcome aggregation caused quenching (ACQ) of molecular dyes for various applications.3,10,11 Real-time monitoring of the RIR procedure of AIEgens helps reveal the rotation-restricted behavior. However, it is challenging and becomes urgent because any factor that leads to RIR could enhance the emission from AIEgens, which could be used to design bright emission systems.
Antenna effect is often observed from lanthanide complexes.12-14 Organic ligand acts as “antenna” to absorb photons and produces triplet state through intersystem crossing from its singlet state.15 The triplet state sensitizes lanthanide $\left(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\right)$ ions to ensure a brighter emission than free ions.16,17 The energy gap between singlet and triplet states of a ligand is critical for efficient emission, whereas the gap between the triplet state of a ligand and the excited state of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions is also strictly limited.18,19 Various lanthanide metal-organic frameworks (Ln-MOFs) have been developed and antenna effect luminescence is well retained with large Stokes shift, bright visible emission, and long lifetime.20-22 MOFs are the hybrid assembly of metal ions and polydentate ligands.23,24 Ligands are fixed in the frameworks with metal ions as ligand-capturers.
After AIEgens, such as tetraphenylethylene, are modified by carboxyl or pyridyl groups, they are used as ligands to prepare emissive MOFs.25-35 Tetraphenylethylene-based emissive MOFs have been used for detection purpose25-30 and as white LED materials.26,27,31 Dincă et al investigated the strain energy and fluorescence behavior of AIE ligands in MOFs.32-34 The rigid structure of MOFs restricts the rotation of benzene rings and achieves higher emission than free ligands.35 However, the above MOFs only show a single emission from the ligands.
Herein we modify tetraphenylpyrazine and tetraphenylethylene, with four carboxyl groups, to form 2, 3, 5, 6-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)pyrazine (L1) and tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)ethylene (L2) (Figure 1). During the formation of MOFs by L1 and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions (including $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+^{\cdot}}$ ), the emission from L1 was enhanced and blueshifted gradually, different from that at fixed wavelength in solid state or poor solvents. Coordination enhances the emission from L1, so the emission is denoted as coordination-induced emission (CIE). CIE is also RRE with the same RIR mechanism as AIE. Different $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions show different emission behaviors. $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions exhibit strong emission and the intensity is enhanced gradually during the formation of L1-Eu MOF. Meanwhile, weak emission is observed from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions and non-emission from ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ ions. Combining with the energy levels of L1, L2, and the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, the antenna effect is validated. CIE and antenna effect emission from L1-Eu MOF are enhanced simultaneously as strong dual emissions. However, only antenna effect emission from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions is exhibited if 1, 4-terephthalic acid (L3) is selected as a ligand. The strong dual emission of L1- Eu MOF is used for ratiometric fluorescence sensing with arginine as a model. The color is easily identified with the naked eyes for visible detection. CIE is a new kind of RRE, so any interaction that results in RIR could enhance the emission from AIEgens, which then are used as building blocks to design and prepare emission systems. MOFs, as a kind of multifunctional matrix, are also used to explore the emission mechanism and to reveal the energy interaction and transfer.
## RESUILTS AND DISCUSSION
Dynamical Monitor of CIE and Antenna Effect Emissions. The fluorescence property of L1 was first investigated in the water/glycerol system. L1 emission was enhanced gradually at a constant $412~\mathrm{nm}$ with increased glycerol content (Figure 2a). Thus, the introduction of hydrophilic carboxyl groups did not disturb obviously the AIE effect of L1. Fluorescence profiles of L1 were further recorded in the presence of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb^{3+}}.$ , and ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ ions in $N,N^{\prime}\cdot$ 一 dimethylformamide (DMF). The emission from L1 was enhanced gradually even if DMF is a good solvent (Figure 2b-2d). The rotatable phenyl rings in L1 are fixed with the coordination procedure to enhance the emission.
Thus, the emission is denoted as CIE, different from AIE in poor solvent. Moreover, the peak emission shifts from 428 nm before reaction to $414~\mathrm{nm}$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ for L1-Eu system. About $14~\mathrm{nm}$ blueshift was observed for L1-Ln systems (Figure $2b-2a$ , 3a, and S1). The same UV-Vis absorption spectra are observed from the L1- $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ systems (Figure S2). Both L1 and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions show high coordinate valence. The plane structure of L1 twists to adapt the coordination for the blue-shifted emission as an additional evidence of CIE. The emission of solid L1 was observed at $430~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3), so the blueshift of L1 was induced by coordination but not simple irregular aggregation in solid state. The characteristic peak of $-{\mathsf{C O O H}}$ at 13 ppm decreased gradually after the addition of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions as revealed from $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra (Figure S4), so the coordination occurred in the system as the hydrogen ions were removed. For the first time, real-time RIR-induced emission is observed through the coordination process. We will further discuss the structure influence of L1 and MOFs on their emission behaviors.
Different emission profiles appear at $500{-}750~\mathrm{nm}$ from different $\mathbf{L1-Ln^{3+}}$ systems. The characteristic emissions of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ ions are well retained with gradually enhanced intensity along with the reaction (Figure 2b). The emission from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions is weak and observed after the image is enlarged (Figure 2c). No emission is observed at 500-750 nm when ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ ions are selected as metal nodes (Figure 2d). The $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ emission enhancement is about 1.3 times higher than that of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions as showed in Figure S5. The emission enhancement of L1 in L1-Gd system is 1.2 times and 1.6 times higher than that of L1-Tb and L1-Eu systems, respectively. In L1-Eu system, the $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ ions enhancement is 1.7 times higher than L1 enhancement (Figure S6). The stronger emission from L1 is related to the weaker emission of the corresponding $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions in L1-Ln systems (Figure 2b2d and 3b). The emission behaviors of L1 and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions motivates us to analyze their energy relationship and transfer.
Energy Variety during Formation of MOFs. Figure 3c illustrates the energy relationship between L1 and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ or ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ ions that induces antenna effect. L1 absorbs photons and is excited to its triplet state $\left(\mathbf{T}_{1}\right)$ through intersystem crossing from its singlet state $(\mathsf{S}_{1})$ . Figure 1b and 1c illustrate its energy level of HOMO and LUMO. The excited state emits its emission directly or sensitizes $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions. According to Reinhold’s empirical rule,16 intersystem crossing is effective when energy gap (ΔE1) between $S_{1}$ and ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ of a ligand is larger than $5,000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ . Based on the UV-Vis absorption of L1 (Figure S7a) and phosphorescence of L1- Gd MOF (Figure S7b), $S_{1}$ and ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ of L1 are $29,412~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (340 nm) and $24{,}096\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ $(415~\mathrm{nm})$ , respectively, with $\Delta\mathrm{E}1$ of $5,316\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . $14\mathrm{nm}$ blueshift of emission from L1 is observed after the reaction. Correspondingly, the $\Delta\mathrm{E}1$ increases to $6,048\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , so we observe variable $\Delta\mathrm{E}1$ in single system during the formation of L1-Ln MOFs. ΔE1 of 5,316-6,048 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is larger than $5,000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , so intersystem crossing of L1 from $\mathsf{S}_{1}$ to ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ is efficient for antenna effect procedure.
The energy gap (∆E2) between ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ of a ligand and the excited state of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions also influences the sensitization efficiency. In L1-Eu system, $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ ranges from 5,919 to 6,596 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Table S1). Previous work illustrated that $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ must be higher than $3{,}500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to achieve irreversible energy transfer to sensitize $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions.36 Thus, $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions are efficiently sensitized by L1. Correspondingly, CIE of L1 and antenna effect emission from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions are dynamically monitored, simultaneously (Figure 2b). The characteristic emissions from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions are observed but lower than that from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions (Figure 2c). $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ between ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ of L1 and excited state of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ranges from 2,864 to $3{,}596\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ during the reaction (Table S1). Energy transfer efficiency is improved with the increased ΔE2. The emission from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions is enhanced gradually, although the emission is very weak. After the formation of rigid topology in L1-Eu and L1- Tb systems, the rotation of benzene rings is considerably restricted.
To further validate the threshold value of ∆E2, the emission behavior of L2 was tested in the presence of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions. The structure and energy level of HOMO/LUMO of L2 are illustrated in Figure 1. Emission from L2 is enhanced and blueshifted gradually as CIE (Figure S8a). S1 of L2 is $28{,}571\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 1),26 while its ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ is 20,499 and 21,459 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ before and after reacting to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions (Figure S8). ∆E1 of L2 during the formation of L2-Eu MOF is 7,112-8,072 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , so its intersystem crossing from $S_{1}$ to ${\mathrm{T}}_{1}$ is efficient. $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ between L2 and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions ranges from 2,949 to 3,959 $\mathbf{cm}^{-1}$ (Table S1). The weak emission from $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ ions is attributed to the limited sensitization, although the emission is enhanced gradually (Figure S8d). Both examples of L1-Tb and L2-Eu illustrate that, when $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ is larger than $3000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}.$ , the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are sensitized for antenna effect emission effectively. $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ ions with higher energy level cannot be sensitized by L2 so that L2- Tb and L2-Gd systems only show single emission from L2 with increased intensity as CIE (Figure S8). Thus, we validate the conditions for the efficient sensitization of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions experimentally and refresh the threshold value of $\Delta\mathrm{E}2$ to $3,000\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ by the dynamical monitoring method. $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, combined with AIE ligands, are used to validate the mechanisms of RRE and antenna effect emission.
To illustrate the merits of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ and AIE ligand for the preparation of emissive MOFs, the fluorescence profile of terephthalic acid (L3) is recorded in the presence of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ ions. The characteristic emissions of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions are well observed with antenna effect procedure (Figure S9a and S9b). However, the emission from L3 decreases gradually with low intensity. Without CIE property, L3 shows a lower emission than L1 and L2 in L1- Gd and L2-Gd systems. Thus, $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, combined with AIE ligands with suitable energy level, could be used to prepare MOFs with strong dual emissions from both CIE and antenna effect.
Preparation and Characterization of L1-Ln MOFs. The colorless MOFs crystals are obtained through the solvothermal reaction (Figure S10). L1 and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , or ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ are mixed in DMF/ethanol/water system with the help of formic acid after heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{h}$ to obtain their crystals (the detail is listed in Supporting Information). The three MOFs show the PXRD patterns that are similar to each other, illustrating their same topological structures (Figure 4a). Thus, L1-Gd MOF is tested to reveal the structure with single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. L1-Gd MOF crystallizes in an orthorhombic space group. The framework contains 8-c rectangular organic linker and $6\mathrm{-c}$ tetranuclear ${\mathrm{Gd}}0_{6}$ cluster (Figure 4b). The (8, 6)-connected
L1-Gd MOF framework forms with an augmented scu topology. Each L1 is connected by eight different ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ ions. Each ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ node is connected by six oxygen atoms, among which four atoms are from carboxyl oxygen of L1 and two atoms are free oxygen. The adjacent ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ nodes are connected by free oxygen to form a zigzag metal chain structure. A diamond-shaped channel is observed with the sizes of 12.1 and $9.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in the frameworks (Figure 4c). The connection between ${\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}$ clusters and L1 constructs a highly porous framework. L1 molecules twist to adapt the MOFs structure. Thus, the dihedral angle between pyrazine core and benzene arms becomes $80.4^{\circ}$ in L1-Gd MOF from $20.1^{\circ}$ of free L1 (Figure 4d). After coordination of L1 to $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, the fluorescence is enhanced as CIE. Meanwhile, the twisted structure of L1 in MOFs achieves the blueshift of 14 nm.
The Fourier transform infrared spectra of L1 and three kinds of MOFs are recorded (Figure S11). The absorption bonds in MOFs at approximately $1{,}710\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ illustrate that L1 is partly deprotonated. The absorption bonds indicate the formation of MOFs by coordinating of −COOH and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions. Thermogravimetric analysis reveals that the MOFs are stable until $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Initially $10\%$ of the residual solvents in channels are lost. Then, the MOFs collapse beyond $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the loss of L1 ligand to remain the metal oxides (Figure S12). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of L1-Eu MOF is $480.42~\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and the pore size is approximately 11.2 Å, which is consistent with the simulated crystal structure (Figure S13). After soaking in water, and $\mathsf{p H}\ 11$ or 3 solutions for $24\ \mathrm{~h},$ the PXRD characteristic peaks of L1-Eu MOF are still stable (Figure S14), indicating that it is stable enough for real application.
Optical Property of L1-Eu MOF. L1 shows a broad absorption between $290~\mathrm{nm}$ and $390~\mathrm{nm}$ in the UV-Vis absorption spectra. However, the absorption shifts to 265- $330~\mathrm{nm}$ in L1-Eu MOF (Figure S15). The emission from L1 is observed at $429{\mathrm{~nm}}_{\cdot}$ , but blueshifts to $415~\mathrm{{nm}}$ after the formation of L1-Eu MOF because of the twisted structure of L1 in MOFs. Moreover, the excitation spectra are the same at approximately $250\mathrm{-}400\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S16 and S17). Strong emission is observed at $630\ \mathrm{nm}$ from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions in L1-Eu MOF with the excitation ranged from $250~\mathrm{nm}$ to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ Thus, the emission from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions is related to L1. That is, L1 is excited and then sensitizes $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions with the antenna effect procedure.
To determine the optimal excitation and quantum yield of L1-Eu MOF, different excitation wavelengths from $240~\mathrm{nm}$ to $320~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is tested. $280~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ excitation provides the strongest fluorescence for L1-Eu MOF (Figure S18a). The 3D emission profile of L1-Eu MOF illustrates the same result that $280\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation is optimal for efficient emission (Figure S18b). The absolute fluorescence quantum yield $(\phi)$ of solid L1 is $12.9\%$ under ambient temperature, but increases to $60.8\%$ observed from L1-Eu MOF. Thus, fluorescence is obviously improved for both CIE and antenna effect in MOFs as strong dual emissions. The lifetime of the emission at $620~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is $134~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ (Figure S19). Given that L1-Eu MOF shows strong dual emissions under single excitation, we explore its fluorescence property for ratiometric fluorescence sensing.
To analyze the structural effect of MOFs on their optical behaviors, we prepared L1-Eu MOF with different morphologies (Figure 5a-5f) by tuning the ratio of DMF or N, $N^{\prime}$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA)/ethanol/water (detail in Table S2). Their PXRD patterns are coincident to each other (Figure 5g). Thus, all of the L1-Eu MOF possess the same topology. The fluorescence profiles are recorded for different L1-Eu MOFs at the same concentration (Figure 5h). The emission from nanosheets MOF is the strongest. Meanwhile rod-like MOF shows higher emission than bulk and spherical MOFs. Then, we explore the AIE property of L1-Eu MOF by comparing the emission from nanospheres and nanosheets in DMF with different water contents (Figure S20). Blue emission from nanosheets is enhanced obviously compared with that from nanospheres with increased water contents. There are uncoordinated L1 exposed on the surface of the nanosheets. With water content increased, the flexible and soft L1-Eu MOF nanosheets aggregated to restrict the intramolecular rotation of L1, so the emission was enhanced. The structure-dependent AIE property is demonstrated because more L1 is exposed on the surface of nanosheets, which have higher AIE activity than nanospheres (Figure S21).
Notably, temperature influences energy transfer efficiency. Thus, temperature-dependent fluorescence is used to verify the AIE property of L1-Eu MOF. The emission from nanosheets increases with the decrease in temperature (Figure S22). At low temperature, the benzene rings’ rotation was restricted, the thermal relaxation decreased partly to improve the emission from L1. L1 is already fixed partly in MOFs, so the fluorescence enhancement (1.7-fold) is lower than that of free L1 (more than 5-fold).37The emission from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions also shows a twofold enhancement. Intermolecular rotation-induced thermal dissipation decreases at low temperature, so energy transfer is improved from L1 to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions, whose emission is enhanced. Thus, structure-dependent AIE property is observed.
Application of L1-Eu MOF for Ratiometric Detection. Ratiometric fluorescence detection improves the signal-tonoise ratio and decreases the effect of environmental factors.38,3A9mino acids are essential to the organism.40 Sensing of amino acids play an important role in disease diagnosis, such as Alzheimers,41 liver cirrhosis,42 and pancreatitis.43 Arginine is associated with a variety of diseases such as cardiovascular and cerebrovascular diseases, hepatopathy, and hyperammonemia.44,45 Thus, a sensitive and selective method for the detection of arginine needs be developed.
L1-Eu MOF nanosheets exhibit blue and red dual emissions under $280\mathrm{nm}$ excitation. The pyrazine core of L1 would interact with the guanidinium group on arginine. Thus, L1-Eu MOF nanosheets were selected as ratiometric fluorescence probe for the detection of arginine. After soaked in arginine solution for $24\mathrm{h}$ , L1-Eu MOF retains its characteristic PXRD peaks, indicating that it is stable enough for the detection of arginine (Figure S23). After the addition of arginine, the fluorescence of L1-Eu MOF nanosheets become stable after 10 minutes (Figure S24). The blue emission from L1-Eu MOF is enhanced, but red emission slightly decreases (Figure 6a). A good linear response is observed between the ratio of $I_{415}/I_{620}$ and the concentration of arginine (Figure S25). L1-Eu MOF is pink, but the color shifts to blue with the increase of arginine content and is easily identified by the naked eyes (Figure 6b and 6c). L1-Eu MOF shows obvious turn-on response without background interference. The detection of limit got with $\mathrm{LOD}{=}\mathrm{kS}_{\mathrm{b}}/a$ , where LOD is the detection of limit; $k=3$ as recommended in spectral analysis, and $S_{\mathrm{b}}$ is the standard deviation of the blank signal and $\pmb{a}$ is the sensitivity of the sensor.46 The detection limit of $15~\mathrm{{nM}}$ is lower than or the same as that of other fluorescence methods.47-49
To explore the selectivity of L1-Eu MOF to arginine, competition experiments are conducted for other amino acids. As shown in Figure S26, the emission responding to arginine is unaffected, except for tryptophan and lysine because of their natural fluorescence at 390 and $430~\mathrm{nm}.$ , respectively. However, their natural fluorescence ensures that they can be easily distinguished (Figure S27).
To better understand the detection mechanism of L1-Eu MOF to arginine, L1, TCPB-Eu MOF prepared with 1, 2, 4, 5- tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene, and $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ are used to record their fluorescence responses to arginine with ${\tt L}2–{\tt E}{\tt u}$ MOF as a reference. The difference between TCPB and L1 is that the pyrazine core is replaced by benzene ring. However, TCPB-Eu MOF exhibits a weak response and L2- Eu shows no response to arginine (Figure S28 and S29), whereas free L1 shows a good linear response to arginine (Figure S30). That is, L1 responds to arginine in L1-Eu MOF. The pKa of arginine is 12.48, so it is positive and acts as electrophile under acidic, neutral, and alkaline conditions. Multiple hydrogen bonds form between lone electron pair of nitrogen on L1 and guanidyl of arginine. No obvious difference was observed between pure L1 and ${\bf L1+}$ arginine from their UV-Vis spectra (Figure S31). Only alkyl hydrogen but no guanidyl hydrogen were observed from $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra of arginine. However, after intermolecular interaction between L1 and arginine, some peaks of arginine red shifted, but that of L1 blue shifted as observed from the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra in Figure S32. Arginine is approximately $6.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in length and $2.{\overset{\sim}{5}}{\AA}$ in width, so it enters the channel of L1-Eu MOF easily. Combining with the hydrogen bond interaction, arginine restricts the rotation of benzene rings in L1 to expand the conjugate range (Figure 6d and 6e) and to enhance the blue emission. The interaction between L1 and arginine does not disturb the energy transfer from L1 to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions. Thus, the red emission is stable and acts as a reference for ratiometric fluorescence sensing and visible detection.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, for the first time, we designed a novel strategy to monitor the emissions from CIE and antenna effect dynamically during the formation of single MOFs. The emission from L1 was enhanced and blueshifted because of the coordination. Correspondingly, the enhanced emission from the antenna effect was revealed in real-time. CIE was observed with the same RIR mechanism as AIE. In combination with the energy variation during the formation of MOFs, we validated and renewed the threshold value of the energy gap between triplet state of the ligands and excited state of the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions to $3,000\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for efficient antenna effect. L1 as ligand and $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ ions as metal nodes were used to prepare MOFs with strong dual emission under single excitation. The strong dual emission achieved ratiometric fluorescence sensing and visible detection with arginine as a model. We anticipate that systems with flexible parts, the same as AIEgens, achieve enhanced fluorescence after being fixed in rigid MOF structure for the development of bright systems. Our results indicate that MOFs can be used as the matrix to explore the fluorescence behavior and mechanism.
